  "app": {
    "title": "BraggFlow — FBG signal processing and figures",
    "summary": "A person creates a project and imports interrogator exports, which can be several files from one run joined in order. They name each channel's sensor with its allowed band and thermal sensitivity, switch processing steps on or off with their parameters, and process. A live plot shows the cleaned signal over the raw one. They drag over a stretch to measure it, place time markers on the plot, and read statistics for each interval between markers. In a second tab they prepare optical spectra, find peaks and valleys, and follow one valley across many files in acquisition order, leaving outliers out and marking events. They come away with processed CSVs with a JSON provenance record, statistics tables as CSV, and a styled publication figure.",
    "task": {
      "text": "Clean and convert raw multichannel optical sensor recordings and spectra into processed signals, interval statistics and publication figures.",
      "verbs": [
        "clean",
        "derive",
        "summarize",
        "explore",
        "compare"
      ]
    },
    "data": {
      "text": "Instrument exports: peak wavelength per sensor channel over time, and optical spectra (intensity against wavelength) acquired one file at a time over a run, plus event markers placed by the person.",
      "item": "one recording file of several sensor channels over time, or one spectrum file in a series",
      "shapes": [
        "time series",
        "table",
        "events",
        "versions"
      ]
    },
    "stage": {
      "text": "cleaning raw interrogator recordings after a test, then analysing them and making figures for a paper",
      "terms": [
        "clean",
        "analyze",
        "report"
      ]
    },
    "mechanism": {
      "text": "A numbered sidebar of files, sensors and toggleable pipeline steps drives a live plot of the processed signal over the raw one. Plot tools brush a stretch to measure it or click to add markers, and the markers drive an interval-statistics table. The spectra tab links a spectrum plot to an evolution plot, where clicking a point opens that spectrum, and a figure tab restyles any of these sources for export. Everything autosaves to a project with undo.",
      "views": [
        "sidebar list",
        "line chart",
        "small multiples",
        "table view",
        "form",
        "summary stats",
        "detail panel"
      ],
      "interactions": [
        "import",
        "brush",
        "select",
        "zoom and pan",
        "toggle view",
        "keyboard shortcuts",
        "inline edit",
        "exclude and refit",
        "search",
        "export",
        "undo",
        "drag to arrange"
      ],
      "patterns": [
        "linked views",
        "overview and detail",
        "small multiples",
        "version history"
      ]
    },
    "field": {
      "text": "fiber Bragg grating sensor signal processing and spectral analysis in optical engineering",
      "disciplines": [
        "engineering"
      ],
      "terms": [
        "fiber bragg grating",
        "optical fiber sensing",
        "photonics"
      ]
    },
    "workflow": {
      "name": "clean multichannel signals, measure intervals between markers, and export a figure",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step": "bring the data in",
          "here": "Create or open a project, then add interrogator or spectrum files by picking, dropping or importing a whole folder. Files from one split run can be joined in an order the person can rearrange.",
          "parts": [
            "project-bar",
            "series-sidebar"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "describe each channel and choose the cleaning steps",
          "here": "Each channel gets a sensor name, an allowed wavelength band, a thermal sensitivity K_T, and the option to hide it. Steps can be toggled with their parameters: invalid-peak marking, deduplication, resampling, null and outlier removal, Savitzky-Golay and moving-average smoothing, Δλ conversion and temperature compensation.",
          "parts": [
            "series-sidebar"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "compute a value from the data",
          "here": "'Processar ▶' runs the recipe and draws the processed signal over the raw one, either one panel per channel or all channels together. A status report lists the invalid samples removed and the k ratio used for each channel.",
          "parts": [
            "series-sidebar",
            "series-plot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "select a stretch and measure it",
          "here": "With 'Medir trecho', dragging over the plot gives mean, σ, min, max, drift and start-to-end difference for every channel. The table can be copied to the clipboard.",
          "parts": [
            "series-plot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "split the run at markers and summarise each interval",
          "here": "The 'Marcar' tool adds or drags marker lines. Statistics are then computed for each interval: mean, drift, noise and a steady-state mean over the last N seconds. Each interval is also compared with the previous or first interval, and the step at each marker is reported. All of it exports as CSV.",
          "parts": [
            "series-plot",
            "interval-stats"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "follow one feature across many items and leave outliers out",
          "here": "Spectra are prepared (transmission, window, smoothing, detrending), then a valley is tracked file by file. Its position comes from a parabola fitted to the bottom, around a reference λ that can follow the drift. Clicking a point opens that spectrum, 'Tirar da evolução' leaves the file out, and events are marked on the plot.",
          "parts": [
            "spectra-view",
            "spectra-evolution"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "export the result",
          "here": "The processed CSV is saved with a JSON provenance sidecar. The Figura tab styles curves from processed series, spectra or the evolution with size presets, fonts, axes and markers, and exports PDF, PNG, SVG, EPS or TIFF or copies the figure to the clipboard.",
          "parts": [
            "figure-view"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distinctive": [
        "Temperature compensation subtracts the reference grating's shift scaled by the ratio of thermal sensitivities, so it needs Δλ conversion turned on first.",
        "Every processed file carries a provenance record: the source file's hash, each step with its parameters, and invalid-sample counts before and after each step.",
        "Each sensor channel has an allowed wavelength band, and readings outside it, lost peaks (zeros) and sudden peak jumps are marked invalid.",
        "A valley is tracked across hundreds of spectra in acquisition order, which is read from file headers or file names. The search window can follow the drift, and events are stored by acquisition instant.",
        "Figure options can be searched, and each option that differs from the loaded style is flagged. Size presets match journal column widths."
      ]
    }
  },
